1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
Okay, moving on to joint, a few basic points before we begin.

2
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:06,440
The first is that a joint is a connection between two bones.

3
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:10,020
We have solid joints, and solid joints are tightly connected,

4
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:14,260
and the purpose of solid joints is to provide structural strength between two bones.

5
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:19,020
And then we have synovial joints, and synovial joints have a joint space that allow for motion.

6
00:00:19,400 --> 00:00:22,880
The rest of this talk is really going to focus on the synovial joints.

7
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,020
Now let's go back and remind ourselves of the anatomy of the joint.

8
00:00:28,020 --> 00:00:31,320
So imagine that these are two bones that need to move against each other.

9
00:00:31,820 --> 00:00:38,280
Remember that each of these bones would, on its surface, have something called an articular cartilage.

10
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:42,240
So this would be the articular cartilage here and articular cartilage here.

11
00:00:42,700 --> 00:00:47,400
And the purpose of this cartilage is to allow for movement against these two bones.

12
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:51,660
Remember that the cartilage is made of type 2 collagen, and that's particularly high yield.

13
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:55,100
Of course, this joint would be surrounded by a joint capsule.

14
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,140
So we can imagine that this is the joint capsule here and joint capsule here.

15
00:00:59,540 --> 00:01:04,660
And then the inner lining of that joint capsule would be lined by something called synovium.

16
00:01:05,100 --> 00:01:07,740
So all of this, for example, would represent synovium.

17
00:01:08,220 --> 00:01:13,020
And the purpose of synovium is to secrete a fluid that is rich in hyaluronic acid.

18
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:18,240
And it's that hyaluronic acid that then goes into the joint space and helps to further lubricate the joint space.

19
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:24,720
So again, just three things, bone, cartilage, and then the synovium, which secrete synovial fluid.

20
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:27,720
Now with that background, we can then go into each of the disorders.

21
00:01:28,300 --> 00:01:30,420
The first disorder is degenerative joint disease.

22
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:34,180
This is when you get progressive degeneration of the articular cartilage.

23
00:01:34,540 --> 00:01:38,900
Recall that the articular cartilage was that layer that was sitting on top of the bone

24
00:01:38,900 --> 00:01:42,680
that was going to help for movement between the bones.

25
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:46,740
Now it's important to note that degenerative joint disease is a wear and tear disease.

26
00:01:46,860 --> 00:01:48,880
Once you understand that, everything else is downhill.

27
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:51,140
The major risk factor is going to be age.

28
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:56,300
Of course, if it's wear and tear, the longer you bang on it, the more likely you are to get the disease.

29
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:58,480
And additional risk factors include obesity.

30
00:01:58,740 --> 00:02:01,480
The more weight you put on the joint, the more likely you are to wear it down.

31
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:02,680
And trauma.

32
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:07,440
If a joint is deformed because of trauma or if a joint is heavily damaged because of trauma,

33
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:09,500
then it can also degenerate.

34
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:12,540
Degenerative joint disease affects a limited number of joints.

35
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:18,240
And it usually involves the hips, the lower lumbar spine, the knees, and the DIP and PIP of the fingers.

36
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:19,580
That's particularly high yield.

37
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:22,520
Both the DIP and the PIP will be involved.

38
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:28,180
Degenerative joint disease classically presents as joint stiffness in the morning that worsens during the day.

39
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:30,860
And this worsens during the day is particularly important.

40
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:34,940
The idea here is that when patients go to sleep at night, the joint gets rest.

41
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:37,140
When they wake up, they feel a little bit stiff.

42
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:42,480
However, when they begin to use the joint, the more they wear and tear it, the more they're going to begin to feel the pain.

43
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:45,880
And so this will worsen during the day, and that's particularly high yield.

44
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:52,120
The pathologic hallmark of degenerative joint disease is disruption of the cartilage that lines the articular surface.

45
00:02:52,540 --> 00:02:56,120
Again, here's the bone, and then you've got this articular surface here.

46
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:58,340
And this cartilage will become disrupted.

47
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,520
Now, when this cartilage becomes disrupted, a few things will happen.

48
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:07,620
Number one, some of these fragments of cartilage will actually fall off into the joint space, and we call those joint mice.

49
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:14,920
Number two, as the cartilage is completely worn away, then bone will be rubbing against bone, up here for example.

50
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:18,600
And that will result in ebernation or polishing of the bone.

51
00:03:18,700 --> 00:03:20,880
So you'll get ebernation of the subchondral bone.

52
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:26,640
Number three is that as you get bone rubbing against bone, you're going to get some reactive bony outgrowths.

53
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,520
And those are called osteophytes.

54
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:32,620
And so you'll see development of osteophytes in the area of damage as well.

55
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:38,300
Now, particularly high yield, the osteophytes classically arise in the DIP and PIP joints of the fingers.

56
00:03:38,600 --> 00:03:43,680
And when this occurs, the patients get enlargement and thickening of the DIP and PIP joints.

57
00:03:43,800 --> 00:03:46,580
And those are called Herberdin and Bouchard nodes.

58
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:51,600
And here's a picture highlighting disruption of the cartilage that lines the articular surface.

59
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:54,120
So this would be the joint space here.

60
00:03:54,120 --> 00:03:57,600
Now, this should have been the normal cartilage coming across like this.

61
00:03:57,960 --> 00:04:00,840
And you can clearly see that this cartilage is disrupted.

62
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:07,420
Now, what will eventually happen is that bone here, for example, will rub against bone here from the other side of the joint, for example.

63
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:10,880
This will result in polishing of the bone, giving you ebernation.

64
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:18,300
And will also eventually result in these bony outgrowths or osteophytes, which could result in enlargement or thickening of the joint.

65
00:04:19,180 --> 00:04:22,140
Rheumatoid arthritis is a chronic systemic autoimmune disease.

66
00:04:22,140 --> 00:04:24,240
It's more commonly seen in middle-aged women.

67
00:04:24,680 --> 00:04:26,680
That's consistent with it being autoimmune.

68
00:04:27,240 --> 00:04:29,500
And it's associated with HLA-DR4.

69
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:32,640
This is particularly high yield for board exam purposes.

70
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:39,100
Now, although it's a systemic disease, the characteristic is really involvement of the joints, hence the term rheumatoid arthritis.

71
00:04:39,760 --> 00:04:45,280
And the hallmark is synovitis, or inflammation of the synovium, which leads to formation of a panis.

72
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:48,140
And a panis, basically, is inflamed granulation tissue.

73
00:04:48,140 --> 00:04:51,420
This is really the highest yield point concerning this disorder.

74
00:04:51,780 --> 00:04:53,240
So let's just illustrate this briefly.

75
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:54,260
Here's your bone.

76
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:57,460
And then, of course, you've got another bone forming the joint.

77
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:00,800
There's articular cartilage sitting on top of both of these.

78
00:05:00,880 --> 00:05:02,060
And then there's a joint capsule.

79
00:05:02,460 --> 00:05:05,820
Now, recall that the lining of the joint capsule is called synovium.

80
00:05:06,240 --> 00:05:11,400
And so what's going to happen in rheumatoid arthritis is that you get a synovitis with inflammation of the synovium.

81
00:05:11,400 --> 00:05:19,320
Now, when you get inflammation of the synovium, that's going to produce a thick layer of granulation tissue because of the inflammation.

82
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:24,560
Now, this granulation tissue, of course, go back to our prior lectures, contains three things.

83
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:26,200
It contains blood vessels.

84
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,480
It contains fibroblasts.

85
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:29,840
But it also contains myofibroblasts.

86
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:32,720
Recall that the myofibroblasts have contractile function.

87
00:05:33,220 --> 00:05:35,420
And they're, of course, what causes a scar to contract.

88
00:05:35,420 --> 00:05:39,880
Well, in the same way that myofibroblasts in this granulation tissue will contract.

89
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:41,940
And when they contract, a few things can happen.

90
00:05:42,360 --> 00:05:47,300
Number one, you'll get fusion of the joint, which is called ankylosis.

91
00:05:47,660 --> 00:05:54,180
Or number two, you can get the dragging of joints in different directions as this granulation tissue contracts.

92
00:05:54,640 --> 00:06:00,180
And so that's another problem that can occur in rheumatoid arthritis where you get the movement of joints in different directions.

93
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:02,720
For example, deviations of joints.

94
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:05,280
For example, different deviations of joints.

95
00:06:05,780 --> 00:06:09,280
And at the same time, because you've got this pannus taking over the articular cartilage,

96
00:06:09,540 --> 00:06:12,360
you can also get destruction of the articular cartilage as well.

97
00:06:12,780 --> 00:06:13,980
So this is really the hallmark.

98
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:15,120
This is the key take-home.

99
00:06:15,780 --> 00:06:18,060
Synovitis with formation of a pannus.

100
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:20,400
With that in mind, let's go back and read the text.

101
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:23,300
The hallmark is synovitis, which leads to formation of a pannus.

102
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:28,060
This results in destruction of the cartilage and ankylosis of the joints.

103
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:32,960
One of the clinical hallmarks of this disorder is that you get morning stiffness that improves with activity.

104
00:06:32,960 --> 00:06:36,640
The idea is that when the patient's sleeping and the joints are sitting at rest,

105
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:41,160
the inflammatory process can easily occur within the joints and begins to damage the joints.

106
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:43,080
So they wake up feeling very stiff.

107
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:47,140
And then as they move around throughout the day, they begin to use the joints,

108
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:50,540
and they're sort of squeezing the joints free of that inflammatory debris,

109
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:53,160
which then results in improvement with activity.

110
00:06:53,640 --> 00:06:58,640
The easy way to remember this is to compare it against a sore throat that you might have from a viral illness.

111
00:06:59,100 --> 00:07:02,260
You're well aware of the fact that when you have a sore throat from a viral illness,

112
00:07:02,540 --> 00:07:04,600
and you're at the, for example, end of the illness,

113
00:07:04,840 --> 00:07:07,600
when you wake up in the morning, you're coughing up a bunch of stuff,

114
00:07:07,660 --> 00:07:08,840
and your throat doesn't feel well.

115
00:07:08,840 --> 00:07:13,800
But then as you begin to speak and use your voice and move around, you feel much better.

116
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:15,820
And so this is the exact same problem.

117
00:07:15,980 --> 00:07:20,620
If they get all this inflammation and inflammatory junk building up in the joints while they're sleeping,

118
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:24,460
and then when they wake up and begin to use the joints, it feels much better.

119
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:28,080
It's also important to note that the involvement of the joints will be symmetric.

120
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:32,100
Both joints are going to be hit by the exact same autoimmune problem.

121
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:35,980
The classic joints that are involved are the PIP joints of the fingers,

122
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:38,640
the wrists, the elbows, the ankles, and the knees.

123
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:41,640
It's important to note that the DIP is usually spared,

124
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:45,500
and that's particularly high yield because recall that in degenerative joint disease,

125
00:07:45,840 --> 00:07:48,720
the PIP and the DIP were both involved.

126
00:07:49,300 --> 00:07:52,520
Here's a picture of a joint from a patient with rheumatoid arthritis.

127
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:57,160
Again, remember that the hallmark is going to be the formation of the pannus,

128
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:00,260
and the pannus contains that granulation tissue, which will then contract.

129
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:05,080
And so you classically get these deformities of the joints where they get pulled one way or another,

130
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:08,660
and that's, again, because the myofibroblasts in the granulation tissue contract,

131
00:08:08,880 --> 00:08:12,280
and when they contract, they then pull the joint one way or another.

132
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:15,600
Additional clinical features include joint space narrowing.

133
00:08:15,820 --> 00:08:20,320
Again, as I've already mentioned, the pannus is going to contract because of the myofibroblast,

134
00:08:20,540 --> 00:08:22,040
which will then narrow the joint space.

135
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:26,020
You'll get loss of cartilage, and that's because the pannus can overlie the cartilage,

136
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:28,460
and the inflammation in the pannus can destroy the cartilage.

137
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:30,320
And you can also get osteopenia,

138
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:35,140
because the inflammatory process will also begin to wear away at the bone underneath the cartilage.

139
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:39,100
Patients will also have signs of symptoms of an inflammatory process,

140
00:08:39,240 --> 00:08:41,460
because, again, this is a systemic autoimmune disease,

141
00:08:41,800 --> 00:08:45,480
so fever, malaise, weight loss, and myalgias will also be seen.

142
00:08:46,020 --> 00:08:49,180
Other clinical features that are important, rheumatoid nodules are present.

143
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:53,280
These are nodules that usually arise in the skin, but can also be present in the visceral organs.

144
00:08:53,820 --> 00:08:56,600
And they're characterized by a central area of necrosis,

145
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:59,060
which is surrounded by epithelioid histiocytes.

146
00:08:59,600 --> 00:09:01,960
So rheumatoid nodules are a classic finding as well.

147
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:06,000
Vasculitis can be seen, and vasculitis can involve multiple organs.

148
00:09:06,480 --> 00:09:10,420
The patient can develop a Baker's cyst, which is swelling of the bursa behind the knee,

149
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:12,920
so they can have a swollen cyst behind the knee.

150
00:09:13,380 --> 00:09:17,020
And then they can develop additional systemic signs, including pleural effusions,

151
00:09:17,400 --> 00:09:20,020
lymphadenopathy, which refers to enlargement of the lymph nodes,

152
00:09:20,300 --> 00:09:22,620
and interstitial lung fibrosis.

153
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:25,220
The laboratory findings are particularly high yield.

154
00:09:25,220 --> 00:09:30,480
The most important is that you're going to have an IgM autoantibody against the FC portion of IgG.

155
00:09:30,860 --> 00:09:34,760
This is called rheumatoid factor, and it's important to note for the purposes of board exams

156
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:42,600
that it's going to be an IgM antibody against the FC portion of IgG.

157
00:09:42,980 --> 00:09:46,460
And again, this is the FC portion, and that's particularly high yield.

158
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:51,120
This is called rheumatoid factor, and rheumatoid factor is actually a marker of tissue damage,

159
00:09:51,220 --> 00:09:53,280
and it's also a marker of disease activity.

160
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:59,200
If you were to stick a needle in one of the joints, you would see neutrophils and high protein in the synovial fluid.

161
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:03,180
Complications of rheumatoid arthritis include anemia of chronic disease.

162
00:10:03,540 --> 00:10:06,020
The idea here is that you've got a chronic inflammatory state,

163
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:10,140
and that's going to result in production of acute phase reactants, such as hepcidin.

164
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:14,540
Hepcidin will then block the ability to use iron that is stored in the macrophages,

165
00:10:14,820 --> 00:10:17,260
and that will result in anemia of chronic disease.

166
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:20,000
We've covered this in detail in your red blood cell lectures.

167
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:23,420
In addition, the patients can also get secondary amyloidosis.

168
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:27,240
Recall that when you have chronic inflammation, you're going to produce, again, acute phase reactants.

169
00:10:27,340 --> 00:10:30,980
One of them is a protein called SAA, which is produced by the liver.

170
00:10:31,500 --> 00:10:37,920
SAA gets converted to AA, and AA will then deposit in various tissues, resulting in a secondary amyloidosis.

171
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:39,640
This is very high yield.

172
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:42,080
They like to ask this on board exams as well.

173
00:10:42,080 --> 00:10:48,600
Now, seronegative spondyloarthropathies are a group of joint disorders that have a few important characteristics.

174
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:53,140
Number one, they lack rheumatoid factor, and that's what we mean by seronegative,

175
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:55,380
that the serum is negative for rheumatoid factor.

176
00:10:55,940 --> 00:11:02,260
Number two, they often involve the axial skeleton, and that's what the spondyloarthropathy refers to,

177
00:11:02,320 --> 00:11:06,760
the spondylo referring to the spine and the large joints of the central skeleton.

178
00:11:06,760 --> 00:11:11,120
And then finally, there's often an association with HLA-B27.

179
00:11:11,860 --> 00:11:15,120
The first of these disorders is called ankylosing spondyloarthritis,

180
00:11:15,320 --> 00:11:18,160
and it involves the sacroiliac joints in the spine.

181
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:22,620
This classically arises in young adults, usually males, and it presents with low back pain.

182
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:27,560
Now, involvement of the vertebral spine over time can lead to fusion of the vertebra,

183
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:29,960
and that's also known as bamboo spine.

184
00:11:30,520 --> 00:11:34,320
And the patients can also have uveitis, which would result in redness of the eye

185
00:11:34,320 --> 00:11:38,900
and then visual abnormalities, such as blurry vision, which could eventually progress to blindness,

186
00:11:39,420 --> 00:11:42,100
and then aortitis, which is inflammation of the aorta.

187
00:11:42,540 --> 00:11:46,460
Now, it's important to note, especially for examinations, that when you get aortitis,

188
00:11:46,640 --> 00:11:49,820
for example, here and here, that's going to result in weakening of the wall,

189
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:52,580
which will increase the risk of formation of an aneurysm.

190
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:57,520
And if were you to form an aneurysm, that could then pull on the aortic valve here and here.

191
00:11:57,520 --> 00:12:01,760
And so one of the consequences of the aortitis in a patient, for example,

192
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:06,820
with ankylosing spondyloarthritis would be that they can develop aortic regurgitation

193
00:12:06,820 --> 00:12:09,660
or regurgitation through the aortic valve.

194
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:14,100
The second of the seronegative spondyloarthropathies is Reiter's syndrome.

195
00:12:14,460 --> 00:12:17,760
This presents as arthritis, urethritis, and conjunctivitis.

196
00:12:18,180 --> 00:12:22,960
And so the classic mnemonic is can't see, can't pee, and can't climb a tree.

197
00:12:22,960 --> 00:12:30,320
Now, this will usually arise in young male adults weeks after a GI or a chlamydia infection.

198
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:34,680
Psoriatic arthritis is an arthritis seen in 10% of cases of psoriasis.

199
00:12:35,100 --> 00:12:37,580
It involves the axial and the peripheral joints.

200
00:12:37,680 --> 00:12:40,560
And one of the key findings with involvement of the peripheral joints

201
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:44,120
is that the DIP of the hands and the feet are commonly affected.

202
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:47,460
And this results in something called a sausage finger or a sausage toe,

203
00:12:47,780 --> 00:12:50,520
which is particularly high yield for board exam purposes.

204
00:12:50,520 --> 00:12:54,060
The next category of arthritis is infectious arthritis.

205
00:12:54,320 --> 00:12:57,420
This is arthritis due to infectious agent, usually bacterial.

206
00:12:58,100 --> 00:13:01,600
The most important and most common overall cause is Neisseria gonorrhea.

207
00:13:01,940 --> 00:13:04,860
This would be classically seen in a sexually active young adult.

208
00:13:05,360 --> 00:13:07,220
The second most common cause is Staph aureus,

209
00:13:07,260 --> 00:13:09,700
and this is classically seen in older children and adults.

210
00:13:10,360 --> 00:13:15,260
Patients with an infectious arthritis will present with involvement of a single joint,

211
00:13:15,480 --> 00:13:16,340
usually the knee.

212
00:13:16,340 --> 00:13:20,160
The joint will be warm and erythematous with a limited range of motion.

213
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:23,040
And the patients will also have systemic signs, such as fever,

214
00:13:23,220 --> 00:13:26,460
an increased wide blood cell count, and an elevated ESR.

215
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:28,740
Now, when a patient has infectious arthritis,

216
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:31,340
you want to be able to detect it quickly and treat quickly

217
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:33,800
because you can easily treat with antibiotics,

218
00:13:34,280 --> 00:13:37,080
and you can then avoid any permanent damage to the joint.

219
00:13:37,680 --> 00:13:39,700
The last two disorders that we're going to cover

220
00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:43,040
are those that are related to deposition of crystals within the joint space,

221
00:13:43,040 --> 00:13:44,360
and the first is gout.

222
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:48,460
In gout, you get deposition of monosodium urate crystals in tissues,

223
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:49,700
especially the joints,

224
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:53,120
and this monosodium urate is actually derived from uric acid,

225
00:13:53,460 --> 00:13:55,260
and so it's due to hyperuricemia,

226
00:13:55,500 --> 00:13:58,220
or high amounts of uric acid present within the blood.

227
00:13:58,680 --> 00:13:59,800
Now, in order to understand gout,

228
00:13:59,860 --> 00:14:01,960
you do need to go back to a little bit of biochemistry,

229
00:14:02,420 --> 00:14:03,720
so let's do a quick overview.

230
00:14:04,380 --> 00:14:06,740
Imagine that this is the lumen of the small bowel,

231
00:14:07,140 --> 00:14:10,660
and this is the enterocyte that normally lines that small bowel lumen.

232
00:14:10,660 --> 00:14:13,280
Now, within the diet will be DNA and RNA,

233
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:17,060
and the DNA and RNA will be broken down into purines and pyrimidines.

234
00:14:17,300 --> 00:14:18,780
We're going to focus here on purines

235
00:14:18,780 --> 00:14:20,800
because it's purines that lead to uric acid,

236
00:14:20,920 --> 00:14:22,540
and it's uric acid that leads to gout.

237
00:14:22,940 --> 00:14:25,800
So the purine breakdown products will be brought into the cell,

238
00:14:26,080 --> 00:14:31,120
and they include adenosine monophosphate and guanine monophosphate.

239
00:14:31,200 --> 00:14:33,640
Of course, adenine and guanine are your key purines.

240
00:14:34,100 --> 00:14:39,060
Adenosine monophosphate will be broken down into hypoxanthine,

241
00:14:39,060 --> 00:14:43,880
and guanine monophosphate will be broken down into guanine,

242
00:14:44,620 --> 00:14:49,160
and then both of these will eventually be broken down into xanthine.

243
00:14:50,300 --> 00:14:53,920
Now, xanthine will then be converted to uric acid,

244
00:14:55,900 --> 00:14:59,960
and this will occur by an enzyme called xanthine oxidase,

245
00:15:00,020 --> 00:15:02,440
and all of this biochemistry is particularly high yield,

246
00:15:02,520 --> 00:15:04,200
and so that's why I'm illustrating it.

247
00:15:04,580 --> 00:15:06,600
Now, uric acid will then be pushed into the blood,

248
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:08,020
will be filtered by the kidney,

249
00:15:08,020 --> 00:15:10,080
and then will be removed from the body.

250
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:12,340
Now, of course, the key problem in gout

251
00:15:12,340 --> 00:15:15,680
is that you have increased uric acid within the blood,

252
00:15:16,040 --> 00:15:19,040
and that increased uric acid in the blood is called hyperuricemia,

253
00:15:19,300 --> 00:15:21,040
and there's two ways by which that could occur.

254
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:24,300
That could occur by pushing too much uric acid into the blood,

255
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:28,280
or it could occur by not filtering the uric acid from the blood,

256
00:15:28,380 --> 00:15:31,540
because remember that uric acid is normally excreted by the kidney,

257
00:15:32,000 --> 00:15:34,000
and so this is the basic biochemical understanding

258
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,440
that you have to have in order to understand the disease.

259
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:40,320
So again, what we say is that gout is deposition of monosodium urate,

260
00:15:40,540 --> 00:15:42,580
which is basically derived from uric acid,

261
00:15:42,700 --> 00:15:44,220
so it's due to hyperuricemia.

262
00:15:44,740 --> 00:15:46,820
In primary gout, which is the most common form,

263
00:15:46,980 --> 00:15:49,020
we don't know what causes the hyperuricemia.

264
00:15:49,480 --> 00:15:50,620
Now, when we say it's unknown,

265
00:15:51,060 --> 00:15:54,600
we know that it's got to be either overproduction or decreased excretion,

266
00:15:54,860 --> 00:15:55,860
and it can be both.

267
00:15:56,180 --> 00:15:57,900
However, we don't know the exact cause,

268
00:15:58,160 --> 00:16:00,260
and this is usually seen in adult males.

269
00:16:00,800 --> 00:16:03,800
In secondary gout, we have hyperuricemia due to a known cause.

270
00:16:03,800 --> 00:16:07,380
For example, it can be seen with leukemia and myeloproliferative disorders.

271
00:16:07,820 --> 00:16:10,120
Now, again, in leukemia and myeloproliferative disorders,

272
00:16:10,220 --> 00:16:11,480
you've got a lot of cell turnover.

273
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:14,360
The cell turnover means you're turning over a lot of nucleus,

274
00:16:14,520 --> 00:16:15,620
and when you turn over a nucleus,

275
00:16:15,740 --> 00:16:17,540
you're going to break down purines and pyrimidines,

276
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:21,420
and the byproduct of breakdown of purines is going to be uric acid.

277
00:16:21,540 --> 00:16:23,680
So you're going to get high levels of uric acid in the blood,

278
00:16:23,980 --> 00:16:25,620
which can then lead to secondary gout.

279
00:16:26,680 --> 00:16:29,580
Another important cause of secondary gout is Lesch-Nihan syndrome.

280
00:16:29,580 --> 00:16:32,500
Let's go back to the whiteboard to illustrate some biochemistry.

281
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:37,480
Remember that important breakdown products from the purine pathway will be AMP and GMP.

282
00:16:38,060 --> 00:16:42,020
The AMP will then be broken down into hypoxanthine,

283
00:16:42,180 --> 00:16:45,220
and the GMP will be broken down into guanine.

284
00:16:45,800 --> 00:16:48,020
And, of course, the hypoxanthine and the guanine

285
00:16:48,020 --> 00:16:50,740
can eventually result in the formation of xanthine,

286
00:16:51,460 --> 00:16:55,360
and the xanthine can eventually result in the formation of uric acid.

287
00:16:55,360 --> 00:16:58,360
Now, it is also possible to take these two products

288
00:16:58,360 --> 00:17:01,340
and to salvage them via something called a salvage pathway

289
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:05,700
so that you can recycle them so that they don't have to then become uric acid.

290
00:17:06,140 --> 00:17:11,520
Now, the key enzyme that does that is called HGPRT,

291
00:17:12,240 --> 00:17:15,760
which is hypoxanthine guanine phosphoriboltransferase.

292
00:17:15,960 --> 00:17:17,660
Now, look at the hypoxanthine guanine,

293
00:17:17,700 --> 00:17:20,940
hypoxanthine guanine phosphoriboltransferase.

294
00:17:20,940 --> 00:17:25,220
And so this enzyme allows for salvage of these two individual molecules.

295
00:17:25,520 --> 00:17:26,920
Now, when you salvage these molecules,

296
00:17:27,060 --> 00:17:28,460
you'll decrease the amount of xanthine,

297
00:17:28,560 --> 00:17:30,280
and you'll decrease the amount of uric acid.

298
00:17:30,680 --> 00:17:34,240
And so you can imagine that if a person is lacking this enzyme,

299
00:17:34,700 --> 00:17:37,820
that's going to result in excess production of uric acid,

300
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:40,400
which will then result in hyperuricemia.

301
00:17:40,960 --> 00:17:43,640
And so these patients with Lesch-Nihan syndrome present with gout.

302
00:17:43,900 --> 00:17:46,880
However, they also have mental retardation and self-mutilation,

303
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:48,720
where they bite their lips and their fingers,

304
00:17:48,720 --> 00:17:51,640
also as a complication of the high levels of uric acid.

305
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:54,960
The third cause of secondary gout would be renal insufficiency.

306
00:17:55,460 --> 00:17:58,820
Now, recall that the kidney has to excrete the uric acid to get rid of it.

307
00:17:58,880 --> 00:18:01,960
And so if there's insufficiency, you won't be able to excrete the uric acid.

308
00:18:02,340 --> 00:18:04,360
That'll result in high levels of uric acid,

309
00:18:04,640 --> 00:18:07,080
which could then result in a secondary gout.

310
00:18:07,760 --> 00:18:10,460
Now, whether the patients have a high level of uric acid

311
00:18:10,460 --> 00:18:13,040
because of a primary problem or a secondary problem,

312
00:18:13,420 --> 00:18:15,420
the clinical findings are relatively the same.

313
00:18:15,420 --> 00:18:18,040
And that is that the patients can present with acute gout.

314
00:18:18,220 --> 00:18:22,940
In acute gout, you get deposition of uric acid within the joints as monosodium urate.

315
00:18:23,320 --> 00:18:26,220
These crystals then trigger an acute inflammatory reaction.

316
00:18:26,380 --> 00:18:29,360
It's high yield to note that the crystals activate the neutrophils.

317
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:33,720
The acute inflammation then results in painful arthritis of the great toe,

318
00:18:34,040 --> 00:18:35,760
which is also known as pedagra.

319
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:39,940
Now, some of the triggers of acute gout include alcohol and consumption of meat.

320
00:18:40,220 --> 00:18:41,400
When a patient consumes meat,

321
00:18:41,400 --> 00:18:44,960
they're going to be bringing in a lot of RNA and DNA into the body,

322
00:18:45,040 --> 00:18:47,600
which can then result in high levels of uric acid.

323
00:18:48,160 --> 00:18:49,640
And when patients consume alcohol,

324
00:18:50,060 --> 00:18:54,420
byproducts of alcohol can compete with uric acid at the kidney to be excreted.

325
00:18:54,680 --> 00:18:57,200
And so therefore, less excretion of uric acid would occur.

326
00:18:57,480 --> 00:19:00,920
And so two important triggers of acute gout are alcohol and consumption of meat.

327
00:19:01,120 --> 00:19:03,980
And that's particularly high yield for the purposes of board exam.

328
00:19:04,600 --> 00:19:06,760
With longstanding high levels of uric acid,

329
00:19:06,960 --> 00:19:08,340
patients can develop chronic gout.

330
00:19:08,340 --> 00:19:11,320
And the hallmark of chronic gout is the formation of TOFI.

331
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:16,080
TOFI are white chalky aggregates of uric acid crystals in soft tissue or joints.

332
00:19:16,700 --> 00:19:19,860
And often it's associated with fibrosis in a giant cell reaction.

333
00:19:20,580 --> 00:19:23,440
In addition, in chronic gout, patients can develop renal failure.

334
00:19:23,840 --> 00:19:27,280
And that's because uric acid can actually deposit within the tubules of the kidney,

335
00:19:27,580 --> 00:19:28,340
damaging the kidney.

336
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:30,140
Here's a picture of TOFI.

337
00:19:30,140 --> 00:19:35,860
These are white chalky deposits of uric acid crystals within the soft tissue, for example.

338
00:19:36,420 --> 00:19:41,100
And remember that you get fibrosis surrounding these white chalky deposits.

339
00:19:42,460 --> 00:19:45,560
Laboratory findings in patients with gout will be hyperuricemia.

340
00:19:45,760 --> 00:19:48,340
Of course, it's due to a high level of uric acid in the blood.

341
00:19:48,340 --> 00:19:52,380
And in addition, sampling of the synovial fluid will show needle-shaped crystals

342
00:19:52,380 --> 00:19:54,860
with negative birefringents under polarized light.

343
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:57,660
Very high yield to know that these are needle-shaped crystals.

344
00:19:58,180 --> 00:20:02,320
And it's also high yield to know that they are negatively birefringent under polarized light.

345
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:06,740
Now, board examiners like you to know what is meant by negative birefringents.

346
00:20:07,060 --> 00:20:10,860
And basically what it means is that when the crystals lay flat, they're yellow.

347
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:14,280
And so the way I remember this is that when they lay low, they're yellow.

348
00:20:15,160 --> 00:20:17,000
And so that's an important thing to be aware of.

349
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:21,880
Board examiners could also ask you what color are the crystals under parallel light.

350
00:20:22,280 --> 00:20:23,320
And the answer is yellow.

351
00:20:23,540 --> 00:20:27,380
And that's easy to remember because there's two L's in both yellow and parallel.

352
00:20:28,020 --> 00:20:29,620
The final disorder is pseudogout.

353
00:20:29,760 --> 00:20:31,100
This clinically resembles gout.

354
00:20:31,200 --> 00:20:32,920
However, it's not gout, hence the word pseudo.

355
00:20:33,380 --> 00:20:35,900
Instead of being due to deposition of monosodium urate,

356
00:20:36,160 --> 00:20:38,640
it's due to deposition of calcium pyrophosphate.

357
00:20:38,940 --> 00:20:41,480
When you stick a needle in the joint and you take the synovial fluid,

358
00:20:41,760 --> 00:20:45,560
you will see rhomboid-shaped crystals instead of the needle-shaped crystals.

359
00:20:45,560 --> 00:20:49,900
And there will be weak positive birefringents under polarized light.

360
00:20:50,440 --> 00:20:52,560
And that concludes our discussion of joint.

